::ENG3_U03_L01_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.R.05] Passage: Repairing the City Steps. The west steps outside the library cracked after years of rain. First, the city blocked the unsafe side with a sign. Next, engineers inspected the stone and found that water had weakened the base. Finally, the city added drainage channels before replacing the steps. The new design prevents rain from collecting under the stone. What structure organizes the passage? {
~Comparison and contrast.#The passage does not compare two options.
=Problem and solution with sequence.#Correct. It presents cracked steps, then ordered steps to solve the problem.
~Question and answer only.#The passage does not use a question-and-answer format.
~Definition and example.#The passage does not define a term.
}

::ENG3_U03_L01_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.R.05] Passage: Repairing the City Steps. The west steps outside the library cracked after years of rain. First, the city blocked the unsafe side with a sign. Next, engineers inspected the stone and found that water had weakened the base. Finally, the city added drainage channels before replacing the steps. The new design prevents rain from collecting under the stone. Which transition helps readers follow the sequence? {
~Cracked after years of rain.#This explains the problem, not the sequence.
~The west steps.#This names the location, not the order.
=Finally.#Correct. "Finally" signals the last step in the sequence.
~Under the stone.#This describes location, not order.
}

::ENG3_U03_L01_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.R.05] Passage: Repairing the City Steps. The west steps outside the library cracked after years of rain. First, the city blocked the unsafe side with a sign. Next, engineers inspected the stone and found that water had weakened the base. Finally, the city added drainage channels before replacing the steps. The new design prevents rain from collecting under the stone. What feature helps identify the topic? {
~The word "stone."#This is a detail, not a feature that previews the topic.
~The word "rain."#This supports cause, but it does not preview the passage as a feature.
~The final sentence only.#The final sentence explains outcome, but the title previews topic.
=The title "Repairing the City Steps."#Correct. The title previews the topic before the passage begins.
}

::ENG3_U03_L01_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.R.05] Passage: Repairing the City Steps. The west steps outside the library cracked after years of rain. First, the city blocked the unsafe side with a sign. Next, engineers inspected the stone and found that water had weakened the base. Finally, the city added drainage channels before replacing the steps. The new design prevents rain from collecting under the stone. Why is the structure effective? {
=It shows the problem, investigation, repair steps, and prevention in order.#Correct. The organization helps readers follow how the city solved the issue.
~It hides the cause until the end for suspense.#The cause appears in the middle, not hidden for suspense.
~It compares two unrelated buildings.#The passage focuses on one set of steps.
~It gives opinions without evidence.#The passage provides specific actions and causes.
}

::ENG3_U03_L01_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.R.05] Passage: Repairing the City Steps. The west steps outside the library cracked after years of rain. First, the city blocked the unsafe side with a sign. Next, engineers inspected the stone and found that water had weakened the base. Finally, the city added drainage channels before replacing the steps. The new design prevents rain from collecting under the stone. Which evaluation is strongest? {
~The passage is good because it has sentences.#This is too vague and does not evaluate structure.
~The passage is confusing because it mentions rain.#Rain explains the cause, so this is not a strong evaluation.
~The passage is ineffective because it has a title.#A title usually helps orient readers.
=The passage is effective because its sequence shows how each action responds to the cracked-step problem.#Correct. This evaluates structure and explains why it works.
}
